;; The name *uiR* is a short and stylized abbreviation for UIR Rewriter, where
;; UIR stands simply for *UI Representation* (*User Interface Representation*).
;;
;; uiR is a kind of *abstract physics* for layouts, where:
;;
;; - Sizes flow up and down (literally).
;; - Horizontal message flow occurs through rightward movement of messages from
;;   `mail/in` through `mail/mid` to `mail/out`, and then to the successor sibling.
;; - Multi-child nodes work by injecting horizontal messages into the leftmost
;;   child and collecting them at the right child, forming a "circle" of decision-
;;   making where both children and their parent participate.
;;
;; There are several major limitations:
;;
;; - uiR rev8 is not self-healing. This means it cannot detect and repair damage
;;   to the underlying uiR. This requires one to recalculate the entire layout
;;   on every change (caching saves us most of the trouble but even caching has limits).
;; - The semantics of live size-change (when parent nodes "rethink" and decide
;;   to alter the `dn-w/h` they have already confirmed and sent down) very hard
;;   to impossible to implement. In practice, we solve this by saving the node
;;   before any sizing has taken place in the pairspart of the node that may trigger
;;   such "rethinking"; we replace the active child with the saved, unsized one
;;   and send the new `dn-w/h` to it.
;;
;; None of these major limitations are seemingly reachable in practice, though.
;; I was not yet able to reach buggy live size-change ("rethinking") with tests,
;; although I know it is possible and the design must accommodate for that.
;;
;; It is important to note that uiR rev8 -- specifically, its ruleset -- is triggered
;; by a rewriter circuit implemented as  *recursive exhR*. For the rules, this means
;; that children nodes (itemspart dicts) are necessarily exhaustively rewritten
;; before the parent is even matched (sometimes phrased as *before the parent
;; receives control*). At the time the parent node receives control, it is sure
;; that the children now everything they may ever know without the parent's help
;; and modifications. Moreover, the slightest change in any child will trigger
;; that child again. Thus, between each parent  rewrite step, there may be an arbitrary
;; amount of child rewrite sub-steps, and so on to the leaves. Without caching, this
;; is disastrous. With caching, it's bearable -- and sometimes feels surprisingly powerful
;; to me; especially in terms of its guarantees.

;; TODO: facilities for connecting viewport to scrollbar (?!)
;; TODO: DwUIR: align: left/center/right on text, then fix selection-button in uir.in
;; TODO: fix naming.
;;
;; The letter λ is just a placeholder for most of these things, although
;; originally it stood for "*l*ength" (as in l = λ, but more distinctive)
;;
;; Most names here are wonky, the most consistent thing here is me being inconsistent.
;; The names themselves are very bad, too.

;; TODO: the notational complexity here is insane. We're getting dangerously close
;; to APL now. The problem that I have though with all of this, is that the notation
;; (and WwML itself) is too noisy visually. The glyphs are indiscriminate. Thus, hard
;; to parse. I'm thinking more and more about going two-dimensional and custom-glyph.
;;
;; Programming languages rarely if ever go 2D; I think it's worth trying, to tune
;; down the noise. Also, I think I'm basically forced to design my own glyphs at
;; this point. Most of these glyphs, not only do they look noisy and out of place,
;; but they also don't look like what they mean. This is especially true of pure
;; shorthand glyphs ("ligatures") -- things like ⍊, which stands for `¦ _`; but
;; one can barely see why. This is the closest glyph I've found and perhaps
;; unsurprisingly it comes from APL!

;;
;; General
;;

;; Vertical message flow.
(_symbol {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ} ⍊ -up-w⫽h: λ') <> {λ': →λ, (λ): ()}
(_symbol {¦ -dn-w⫽h: λ'} ⍊ dn-w⫽h: ±λ) <> {λ': →λ, (λ): ()}

;; Horizontal message flow.
⟨{¦ mail/out: M←[m_*]} {¦ -mail/in: m'}⟩ <> {m': →m, (M): ()}

;; Positioning trigger flows from the parent to the child.
(_symbol {¦ -position_} ⍊ position) <> {position: true}

;; Nodes that want to define up-w/h only when their size changed can
;; do so by submitting it through up-⸨w,h⸩/new. This rule system will
;; maintain the necessary state and do the comparisons.
{¦ up-⸨w,h⸩/old: λ'_, up-⸨w,h⸩/new: λ←λ'_} <> {(λ): ()}
{¦ up-⸨w,h⸩/old: λ'_, up-⸨w,h⸩/new: λ_, -up-w⫽h: λ'} <> {λ': →λ, (λ): ()}
{¦ -up-⸨w,h⸩/old: λ', up-⸨w,h⸩/new: λ_, -up-w⫽h: λ'} <> {λ': →λ, (λ): ()}

;;
;; root
;;

;; There is no one to take up-w/h from root, so burn them.
{¦ root up-w⫽h: ±λ} <> {(λ): ()}

;; When root nodes receive control unrelated to up-w/h, treat that as
;; request for phase change; switch to the `position` phase.
;;
;; NOTE: we separate sizing and positioning into two distinct phases to avoid
;; needless position recomputation in case of constraint refinement. In other
;; words, once definite sizes are determined, position once -- instead of
;; positioning on every size change.
{¦ root -up-w -up-h -position_} <> {position: true}

;;
;; content
;;

;; Bounce back up-w/h as dn-w/h.
(content {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ -dn-w⫽h: λ'} ⍊ x⫽y -up-w⫽h: λ') <> {λ': →λ, (λ): ()}

;; Reject dn-w/h since `content` is used to force content-sizing even if
;; the parent disagrees.
(content _ ⍊ x⫽y dn-w⫽h: ±λ) <> {(λ): ()}

;;
;; text
;;

;; Make a request to DwUIR to get the content size of the text without
;; any constraints.
(text ⍊ -intrinsic-status -dw-request_)
  <> {dw-request: (measure intrinsic-w intrinsic-h status: intrinsic-status)}

;; Propagate width from DwUIR's intrinsic-size as up-w unconditionally.
(text ⍊ -up-w: λ' intrinsic-w: ±λ) <> {λ': →λ, (λ): ()}

;; Ask DwUIR to define up-h for us once dn-w is known. Now that we know
;; dn-w, we can define final-w of the text (a property required by DwUIR).
(text ⍊ dn-w: ±w ⋮final-w -up-h -dw-request_)
  <> {dw-request: (measure →w up-h status: intrinsic-status), final-w: →w, (w): ()}

;; Similarly, define final-h based on dn-h once it is know. final-h is also
;; a property required by DwUIR to draw text.
(text ⍊ dn-h: ±h ⋮final-h) <> {final-h: →h, (h): ()}

;;
;; Sized nodes effectively request sizing (final-w/h) from UIR. This may be
;; needed if they rely on sized DwUIR features such as `pivot: true`. Serve
;; the sizing info.
;;

(sized _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: ±λ ⋮final-w⫽h) <> {final-w⫽h: →λ}

;;
;; window
;;

;; The window node will force a certain width/height, presumably the actual
;; (system) window size. You can always opt out by making the content node
;; a descendant; if not for this rule, there would have been no way to
;; "opt in" otherwise.
(window {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ -dn-w⫽h_} ⍊ ±⸨width,height⸩)
  <> {dn-w⫽h: →⸨width,height⸩, (λ): ()}

;;
;; rect, svg
;;
(⟦rect,svg⟧ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: ±λ ⋮final-w⫽h) <> {final-w⫽h: →λ, (λ): ()}

;; For rectangles with image fill, we will attempt to query the intrinsic
;; size of the image and use that as our up-w/h proposal.
(rect ⍊ fill: [image _] -intrinsic-status -dw-request_)
  <> {dw-request: (measure intrinsic-w intrinsic-h status: intrinsic-status)}

;; Ask DwUIR about the svg's size. If possible, DwUIR will reply through rewriting
;; with the SVG's intrinsic size, which we'll use as the proposal up-w/h.
(svg ⍊ -intrinsic-status -dw-request_)
  <> {dw-request: (measure intrinsic-w intrinsic-h status: intrinsic-status)}

;; DwUIR may very well return ugly floating point numbers for size; so we
;; use ceil to remove that.
(⟦rect,svg⟧ ⍊ intrinsic-w: ±iw intrinsic-h: ±ih -up-w_ -up-h_)
  <> {up-w: $'(ceil →iw), up-h: $'(ceil →ih), (iw): (), (ih): ()}

;;
;; viewport
;;

;; Save up-w/h under content-w/h for DwUIR. Properties like pan-x-side
;; require us to know the size of what's inside the viewport so that we can
;; adjust the pan.
(viewport {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ} ⍊ -up-w⫽h: λ' ⋮content-w⫽h)
  <> {up-w⫽h: →λ, content-w⫽h: →λ, λ': →λ, (λ): ()}

;; Save dn-w/h under final-w/h for DwUIR. Viewports need to know their
;; "outer" size to build the clipping rect.
(viewport _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: ±λ ⋮final-w⫽h) <> {final-w⫽h: →λ}

;;
;; padding
;;

;; Initialize
(padding _ ⍊ pl⫽t⋮ 0 pr⫽b⋮ 0 -px⫽y_) <> {px⫽y: $'(+ →pl⫽t →pr⫽b)}

;; Alter up-w/h flow.
(padding {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ} ⍊ ±px⫽y -up-w⫽h: λ') <> {λ': $'(+ →λ →px⫽y), (λ): ()}
(padding {¦ -dn-w⫽h: λ'} ⍊ ±px⫽y dn-w⫽h: ±λ) <> {λ': $'(- →λ →px⫽y), (λ): ()}

;; Offset the child by pl/pt.
(padding {¦ dl⫽t⋮ 0} ⍊ position ±pl⫽t -◇_) <> {dl⫽t: $'(+ →dl⫽t →pl⫽t), ◇: true}

;;
;; limit
;;

;; Intercept and release up-w/h.
(limit {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ} ⍊ -up-w⫽h: λ') <> {λ': (arg →λ), (λ): ()}
(limit _ ⍊ up-w⫽h: Λ←[arg ±λ]) <> {Λ: →λ}

;; Intercept and release dn-w/h.
(limit _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: ±λ) <> {λ: (arg →λ)}
(limit {¦ -dn-w⫽h: λ'} ⍊ dn-w⫽h: Λ←[arg ±λ]) <> {λ': →λ, (Λ): ()}

;; Apply constraints on up-w/h.
(limit _ ⍊ up-w⫽h: (arg ±λ ⍊ -◇_) ±⟦min,max⟧-w⫽h)
  <> {λ: $'(⟦max,min⟧ →⟦min,max⟧-w⫽h →λ), ◇: true}

;; Apply max-w/h constraints on dn-w/h. This seems the right thing to do;
;; even if the parent gives us more size we'll still adhere to the child's
;; own max width restriction.
(limit _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: (arg ±λ ⍊ -◇) ±max-w⫽h)
  <> {λ: $'(min →λ →max-w⫽h), ◇: true}

;;
;; percents
;;

;; Intercept and release dn-w/h.
(percents _ ⍊ ±dn-w⫽h: λ) <> {λ: (arg →λ)}
(percents {¦ -dn-w⫽h: λ'} ⍊ dn-w⫽h: Λ←[arg ±λ]) <> {λ': →λ, (Λ): ()}

;; Scale.
(percents _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: (arg ±λ ⍊ -◇_) w⫽h: scale←(%number 0 <= _ <= 1))
  <> {λ: $(round (* →λ →scale)), ◇: true}

;;
;; x/y-signal
;;

;; Save up-w/h under desired-w/h.
(x⫽y-signal {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ} ⍊ ⋮desired-w⫽h -up-w⫽h: λ')
  <> {λ': →λ, desired-w⫽h: →λ, (λ): ()}

;; Intercept dn-w/h.
(x⫽y-signal _ ⍊ ±dn-w⫽h: λ) <> {λ: (arg →λ)}
(x⫽y-signal {¦ -dn-w⫽h: λ'} ⍊ dn-w⫽h: Λ←[arg ±λ]) <> {λ': →λ, (Λ): ()}

;; Check if intercepted dn-w/h fits.
(x⫽y-signal _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: (arg ±dn-w⫽h ⍊ -fits_) ±desired-w⫽h fitness⋮ true ±threshold)
  <> {fits: $(and (>= →dn-w⫽h →desired-w⫽h) (>= →dn-w⫽h →threshold))}
(x⫽y-signal _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: (arg ±dn-w⫽h ⍊ -fits_) ±desired-w⫽h fitness: false ±threshold)
  <> {fits: $'(>= →dn-w⫽h →threshold)}
(x⫽y-signal _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: (arg ±dn-w⫽h ⍊ -fits_) ±desired-w⫽h -threshold)
  <> {fits: $'(>= →dn-w⫽h →desired-w⫽h)}

;; If it does not fit, signal up and reject dn-w/h.
(x⫽y-signal _ ⍊ dn-w⫽h: A←(arg ±dn-w⫽h ⍊ fits: false) id_ -up-signal_)
  <> {up-signal: →id, (A): ()}

;; `up-signal`s flow up.
⟨{¦ up-signal: sig_} ⍊ -up-signal: sig'⟩
  <> {sig': →sig, (sig): ()}

;;
;; switch
;;

;; We currently only support a binary switch; relying on the client's strategic
;; placement of those for efficiency. Nesting switches or having huge lg/sm in
;; switches is not expected to be particularly efficient at the moment.

;; TODO: switch picks `to` irrevokably. This should be fixed once it
;; is reachable with tests & practically significant.

(switch `N ⍊ -mode_ from_) <> {mode: from, N: →from}

;; A switch catches up signals and switches to `to`.
(switch N_ ⍊ mode_: from to_ on: id_ up-signal: sig←id_)
  <> {N: →to, mode: to, (sig): ()}

;;
;; floating
;;

(floating {¦ up-w⫽h: ±λ -dn-w⫽h: λ'} ⍊ x⫽y) <> {λ': →λ, (λ): ()}
(floating _ ⍊ x⫽y dn-w⫽h: ±λ) <> {(λ): ()}

;;
;; align
;;

;; Update lax-w/h as up-w/h flows up from the child.
(align {¦ ±up-w⫽h} ⍊ x⫽y: _number ⋮lax-w⫽h -up-w⫽h: lax-w⫽h)
  <> {lax-w⫽h: →up-w⫽h, (up-w⫽h): ()}

;; Update strict-w/h as dn-w/h flows down to the child.
(align {¦ -dn-w⫽h: strict-w⫽h} ⍊ x⫽y: _number ±lax-w⫽h ±dn-w⫽h ⋮strict-w⫽h ⋮out-w⫽h)
  <> {out-w⫽h: →dn-w⫽h, strict-w⫽h: $'(min →lax-w⫽h →dn-w⫽h), (dn-w⫽h): ()}

;; Strict positioning.
(align {¦ dl⫽t⋮ 0} ⍊ -◇_ position ±x⫽y ±strict-w⫽h ±out-w⫽h)
  <> {dl⫽t: $(+ →dl⫽t (round (- (* →out-w⫽h →x⫽y) (* →strict-w⫽h →x⫽y)))), ◇: true}

;; Lax positioning.
(align {¦ dl⫽t⋮ 0} ⍊ -◇_ position lax ±x⫽y ±lax-w⫽h ±out-w⫽h)
  <> {dl⫽t: $(+ →dl⫽t (round (- (* →out-w⫽h →x⫽y) (* →lax-w⫽h →x⫽y)))), ◇: true}

;;
;; Message flow control
;;

;; As the universal default, simply forward any incoming messages
;; to mail/mid.
{¦ mail/in: M_ -mail/mid_} <> {mail/mid: →M, (M): ()}

;; Once no rules remain that have something to do with mail/mid,
;; we forward it to mail/out.
{¦ mail/mid_ -mail/out_} <> {mail/out: →mail/mid, (mail/mid): ()}

;;
;; `metrics` event
;;
;; metrics events are sent by x/y/z-stack's to assess the state of their children.
;; The measurement is generic. We do not differentiate between the different kinds
;; of stacks at this point.

;; Update x/y-stride from up-w/h.
{¦ mail/mid: [metrics _] ±up-w⫽h ⋮x⫽y-stride}
  <> {x⫽y-stride: →up-w⫽h, (up-w⫽h): ()}

;; Max of widths/heights.
▪ {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ w⫽h-max_: ?} ⍊ -▢_) -up-w⫽h shrink: (%- (%any true w⫽h)) ±x⫽y-stride}
    <> {w⫽h-max: →x⫽y-stride, ▢: true}
  {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ ±w⫽h-max} ⍊ -▢_) -up-w⫽h shrink: (%- (%any true w⫽h)) ±x⫽y-stride}
    <> {w⫽h-max: $'(max →w⫽h-max →x⫽y-stride), ▢: true}

;; Sum of widths/heights excluding fr children.
▪ {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ w⫽h-lo_: ?} ⍊ -▢_) -fr -up-w⫽h ±x⫽y-stride}
    <> {w⫽h-lo: →x⫽y-stride, ▢: true}
  {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ ±w⫽h-lo} ⍊ -▢_) -fr -up-w⫽h ±x⫽y-stride}
    <> {w⫽h-lo: $'(+ →w⫽h-lo →x⫽y-stride), ▢: true}

;; Sum of widths/heights including fr children.
▪ {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ w⫽h-hi_: ?} ⍊ -▢_) -up-w⫽h ±x⫽y-stride}
    <> {w⫽h-hi: →x⫽y-stride, ▢: true}
  {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ ±w⫽h-hi} ⍊ -▢_) -up-w⫽h ±x⫽y-stride}
    <> {w⫽h-hi: $'(+ →w⫽h-hi →x⫽y-stride), ▢: true}

;; Sum of frs.
▪ {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ ±fr-sum} ⍊ -▢_) ±fr}
    <> {fr-sum: $'(+ →fr-sum →fr), ▢: true}

;; No. of participants: if I modified the metrics then increment
;; the participants count.
▪ {¦ mail/mid: (metrics {¦ N⋮ 0} ¦ (%not ()) -▢_)}
    <> {N: $'(+ →N 1), ▢: true}

;;
;; Stack events
;;

;; If a child reports up-w/h that is exactly the same as the previous one,
;; x/y/z-stack won't reply to avoid infinite feedback. However, the child itself
;; may rely on the parent relaying the up-w/h. Therefore, if the child sees
;; that its x/y-stride is exactly the same as up-w/h that it reports, it will
;; reply to itself with det-w/h.
{¦ x⫽y-stride: λ_ up-w⫽h_: λ_ ±det-w⫽h -dn-w⫽h_}
  <> {dn-w⫽h: →det-w⫽h, (up-w⫽h): ()}

;; x/y-resize

;; Update det-w/h of fractionally-sized node.
{¦ mail/mid: (x⫽y-resize {¦ fr-sum_number: (%not 0)} ±rem-w⫽h ⍊ -▢_) ⋮det-w⫽h ±fr}
  <> {det-w⫽h: $(round (* →rem-w⫽h (/ →fr →fr-sum))), ▢: true}
;; Update det-w/h of exact-sized node.
{¦ mail/mid: (x⫽y-resize {¦ ±x⫽y-limit} _ ⍊ -▢_) ±x⫽y-stride ⋮det-w⫽h}
  <> {det-w⫽h: $'(min →x⫽y-limit →x⫽y-stride), ▢: true}
;; Send det-w/h as dn-w/h.
{¦ mail/mid: (x⫽y-resize _ _ ⍊ ▢ -◇_) -dn-w⫽h_ ±det-w⫽h}
  <> {dn-w⫽h: →det-w⫽h, ◇: true}

;; x/y-expand

{¦ mail/mid: (x⫽y-expand _ ±λ ⍊ -◇_) ⋮det-w⫽h -dn-w⫽h: det-w⫽h}
  <> {det-w⫽h: →λ, ◇: true}

;; x-place

;; If width/height is determinate on the child, use it to advance
;; delta; offset child by delta, and pass positioning trigger.
{¦ mail/mid: [x⫽y-place ±l⫽t ±gap] ±det-w⫽h dl⫽t⋮ 0 -position_}
  <> {dl⫽t: $'(+ →l⫽t →dl⫽t), l⫽t: $'(+ →l⫽t →gap →det-w⫽h), position: true}

;; If width/height is indeterminate, simply offset the child by delta;
;; equip it with positioning trigger and move on.
{¦ mail/mid: [x⫽y-place ±l⫽t _] dl⫽t⋮ 0 -position_}
  <> {dl⫽t: $'(+ →l⫽t →dl⫽t), position: true}

;; z-place
{¦ mail/mid: [z-place] -position_} <> {position: true}

;; Register it when a child has a width/height to tell.
{¦ up-w⫽h mail/mid: [(%any x-resize y-resize x-expand y-expand) {¦ -invalid_} _*]}
  <> {invalid: true}

;;
;; Rules common to x/y/z-stack.
;;
;; We use %any to lessen their specificity on purpose, so that *-stack's
;; can override them.

;; Place the metrics to the pairspart.
((%any x-stack y-stack z-stack) _* {¦ mail/out_: [metrics m_]} ⍊ metrics_)
  <> {(mail/out): (), metrics: →m}

;; Invalidate metrics.
((%any x-stack y-stack z-stack) _* {¦ mail/out_: [_ {¦ invalid} _*]} ⍊ metrics_)
  <> {(metrics): (), (mail/out): ()}
[(%any x-stack y-stack z-stack) _* {¦ mail/out_}]
  <> {(mail/out): ()}

;;
;; Rules common to x/y-stack.
;;

;; Enhance resulting metrics with gap width sum.
((%any x-stack y-stack) _* {¦ mail/out: [metrics {¦ -gaps_ ±N}]} ⍊ gap⋮ 0)
  <> {gaps: $(* (- →N 1) →gap)}

;;
;; x/y-stack
;;

;; Request metrics for x-stack.
(x-stack {¦ -mail/in_} _+ ⍊ -metrics_)
  <> {mail/in: (metrics {w-lo: 0, w-hi: ?, h-max: ?, fr-sum: 0}), metrics: wait}

;; Request metrics for y-stack.
(y-stack {¦ -mail/in_} _+ ⍊ -metrics_)
  <> {mail/in: (metrics {w-max: ?, h-lo: 0, h-hi: ?, fr-sum: 0}), metrics: wait}

;; Calculate main candidate.
(x⫽y-stack _* {¦ mail/out: [metrics {¦ ±w⫽h-hi ±gaps}]} ⍊ -up-⸨w,h⸩/new: w⫽h)
  <> {w⫽h: $(+ →w⫽h-hi →gaps)}

;; Calculate cross candidate.
(x⫽y-stack _* {¦ mail/out: [metrics {¦ ±h⫽w-max}]} ⍊ -up-⸨h,w⸩/new: h⫽w)
  <> {h⫽w: →h⫽w-max}

;; Trigger main-resize once main is known.
(x⫽y-stack {¦ -mail/in_} ⁑ metrics_: {¦ ±w⫽h-lo ±gaps} ±dn-w⫽h)
  <> {mail/in: (x⫽y-resize $'(union →metrics {x⫽y-limit: →dn-w⫽h})
                           $'(- →dn-w⫽h →w⫽h-lo →gaps)),
      (dn-w⫽h): ()}

;; Trigger cross-expand once cross is known.
(x⫽y-stack {¦ -mail/in_} ⁑ metrics_ ±dn-h⫽w)
  <> {mail/in: (y⫽x-expand →metrics →dn-h⫽w), (dn-h⫽w): ()}

;; Trigger positioning of children.
(x⫽y-stack {¦ -mail/in_} ⁑ position⁺ gap⋮ 0)
  <> {mail/in: (x⫽y-place 0 →gap), position: false}

;;
;; z-stack
;;

;; Request metrics.
(z-stack {¦ -mail/in_} _+ ⍊ -metrics_)
  <> {mail/in: (metrics {w-max: ?, h-max: ?}), metrics: wait}

;; Assign size candidate.
(z-stack _* {¦ mail/out: [metrics {¦ ±w⫽h-max}]} ⍊ -up-⸨w,h⸩/new: w⫽h)
  <> {w⫽h: →w⫽h-max}

;; Trigger expand once size is known.
(z-stack {¦ -mail/in_} ⁑ metrics_ ±dn-w⫽h)
  <> {mail/in: (x⫽y-expand →metrics →dn-w⫽h), (dn-w⫽h): ()}

;; Trigger positioning of children.
(z-stack {¦ -mail/in_} ⁑ position⁺)
  <> {mail/in: (z-place), position: false}

;;
;; x/y-log
;;

;; For children that know their up-w already, x-log simply records that up-w.
{¦ mail/mid: (x⫽y-log (_* `T) ⍊ -▢_) ±up-w⫽h} <> {T: →up-w⫽h, ▢: true}
;; For children that tell they're fr but do not know their size, x-log
;; records that as flexible size, indicated by `~`.
{¦ mail/mid: (x⫽y-log (_* `T) ⍊ -▢_) -up-w⫽h fr} <> {T: ~, ▢: true}
;; For other children, x/y-log records unknown size, indicated by `?`.
{¦ mail/mid: (x⫽y-log (_* `T) ⍊ -▢_)} <> {T: ?, ▢: true}

;;
;; wrap-stat
;;

;; Zip through the log, collecting statistics. We're interested in N,
;; the number of known-participant children (to calculate accumulated gap),
;; and Sp, the total size of participant children.
[wrap-stat `L R←(_*)] <> {L: ()}
(wrap-stat (_* `l) (r←~ _*) ⍊ Np⋮ 0) <> {Np: $'(+ →Np 1), l: →r, (r): ()}
[wrap-stat (_* `l) (r←? _*)] <> {l: →r, (r): ()}
(wrap-stat (_* `l) (±w _*) ⍊ Np⋮ 0 Sp⋮ 0)
  <> {Np: $'(+ →Np 1), Sp: $'(+ →Sp →w), l: →w, (w): ()}

;;
;; x/y-wrap
;;

;; Copy nodes out of the rewrite-protected pairspart into rewriteable
;; itemspart. Let nodes rewrite themselves until some of them can tell
;; their up-w (if they can of course).
(x⫽y-wrap `T ⍊ -state_ nodes_: (_+)) <> {(T): →nodes, state: initial}

;; If gap is given and no gap-x/y, assume the client wants both values
;; to be the same.
(x⫽y-wrap _* ⍊ state: initial ±gap -gap-x_ -gap-y_)
  <> {gap-x: →gap, gap-y: →gap}

;; Once that is the case, initiate log.
(x⫽y-wrap {¦ -mail/in_} _* ⍊ state_: initial)
  <> {mail/in: (x⫽y-log ()), state: log}

;; After the log is known, stat it by instantiating the wrap-stat zipper.
(x⫽y-wrap (%group children_ _* {¦ mail/out: [x⫽y-log log_]}) ⍊ state_: log)
  <> {children: (wrap-stat →log), state: stat}

;; NOTE: x/y-wrap must have at least one sized child for Sp/N to be defined,
;; otherwise, x/y-wrap itself will be unsized (thus most likely floating).
(x⫽y-wrap N←(wrap-stat log_ () ¦ stats_) ⍊ state_: stat -log: log-dst -stats: stats-dst)
  <> {(N): (), log-dst: →log, stats-dst: →stats, state: sizing}

;; Calculate up-w/h.
(x⫽y-wrap ⍊ state_: sizing gap-x⫽y⋮ 0 stats: {¦ ±Sp ±Np} -up-w⫽h_)
  <> {state: sized, up-w⫽h: $(+ →Sp (* →gap-x⫽y (- →Np 1)))}

;; Consume dn-w/h until in sized state.
(x⫽y-wrap ⁑ ±dn-w⫽h) <> {(dn-w⫽h): ()}

;; Construct wrapper on dn-w/h in sized state.
(x⫽y-wrap children_* ⍊ state: sized ⋮limit ±dn-w⫽h)
  <> {children: (wrapper main: x⫽y-stack cross: y⫽x-stack), limit: →dn-w⫽h, (dn-w⫽h): ()}

;; Respond with limit to any up-w/h to avoid infinite loops (children
;; x/y-stack's reporting their size leads to rewrap leads to new x/y-stack's
;; reporting their sizes and so on).
(x⫽y-wrap {¦ ±up-w⫽h -dn-w⫽h_} ⍊ ±limit) <> {dn-w⫽h: →limit, (up-w⫽h): ()}

;;
;; wrapper
;;

;; Initialize from the enclosing x/y-wrap.
(x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -limit: limit') ⍊ ±limit) <> {limit': →limit}
(x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -log: log') ⍊ log_) <> {log': →log}
(x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -nodes: nodes') ⍊ nodes_) <> {nodes': →nodes}
(x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -min-span: min-span') ⍊ min-span_) <> {min-span': →min-span}
(x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -max-span: max-span') ⍊ max-span_) <> {max-span': →max-span}

;; Define gap-main and gap-cross.
(x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -gap-main_) ⍊ ±gap-x⫽y) <> {gap-main: →gap-x⫽y}
(y⫽x-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -gap-cross_) ⍊ ±gap-x⫽y) <> {gap-cross: →gap-x⫽y}

;; Transition into an initialized state by defining rem (remainder)
;; and span (no. of nodes on the main axis).
[x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -rem_ -span_ ±limit)] <> {rem: →limit, span: 0}

;; Sink rule that fires when there's nothing left to initialize.
[x⫽y-wrap (wrapper ⍊ -ready_)] <> {ready: true}

;; Surround with cross axis.
[x⫽y-wrap C←(wrapper ⍊ ready cross_ gap-cross⋮ 0)]
  <> {C: (→cross →C gap: →gap-cross)}

;; Surround with main axis.
[cross_ _* C←(wrapper ⍊ main_ cross_ gap-main⋮ 0)]
  <> {C: (→main →C gap: →gap-main)}

;; Propose to expel left. This rule votes yes unconditionally.
[main_ _* (wrapper ⍊ main_ ±rem ±span log: (±w _*) -proposal_)]
  <> {proposal: (prepare {irem: $'(- →rem →w), span: $'(+ →span 1), accept: true})}
[main_ _* (wrapper ⍊ main_ ±span log: (~ _*) -proposal_)]
  <> {proposal: (prepare {irem: →rem, span: $'(+ →span 1), accept: true})}
[main_ _* (wrapper ⍊ main_ log: (? _*) -proposal_)]
  <> {proposal: (prepare {accept: true})}

;; Subtract gap-x if necessary from intrinsic rem to obtain rem.
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: (prepare {¦ ±irem -rem_}) span: 0)
  <> {rem: →irem}
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: (prepare {¦ ±irem -rem_}) gap-main⋮ 0)
  <> {rem: $'(- →irem →gap-main)}

(wrapper ⍊ proposal_: (prepare p_)) <> {proposal: →p}

;; These rules vote on the proposal to expel left also. Note how each
;; proceeds only if all other accepted with the `accept: true` constraint.
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: {¦ ±rem accept⁺ -◇_})
  <> {accept: $'(>= →rem 0), ◇: true}
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: {¦ ±span accept: true -min-sat_} min-span_: (%number (whole _) > 0))
  <> {min-sat: $'(> →span →min-span)}
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: {¦ ±span accept: true -max-sat_} max-span_: (%number (whole _) > 0))
  <> {max-sat: $'(<= →span →max-span)}

;; If min/max unsat, vote against proposal. Otherwise, either min/max
;; is missing or it is sat.
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: {¦ accept_: true min-sat: false}) <> {accept: false}
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: {¦ accept_: true max-sat: false}) <> {accept: false}

;; These rules have deliberately low specificity vs. the voting rules.
;; They announce the result of voting when no more voters remain.
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: p←{¦ accept: true}) <> {p: (accepted →p)}
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: p←{¦ accept: false}) <> {p: (declined →p)}

;; If min unsat in declined, convert to accepted (aka force even if overflows
;; when min unsat).
(wrapper ⍊ proposal: P←(declined p←{¦ min-sat: false})) <> {P: (accepted →p)}

;; Force accept if span is 0 (the first item doesn't fit in the main axis).
;; If we don't do this we'll loop forever if that happens.
(wrapper ⍊ span: 0 proposal: P←(declined p_)) <> {P: (accepted →p)}

;; Merge accepted.
C←(wrapper ⍊ proposal: P←(accepted {¦ rem: ±rem' span: ±span'}) ±rem ±span nodes: (N_ _*) log: (record_ _*))
  <> {(C): (↑N0 ↑C), (P): (), rem: →rem', span: →span', (N): ($tr N0 ()), (record): ()}

;; Destroy declined and exit the main stack.
S←[main_ _* C←(wrapper ⍊ main_ proposal: P←(declined _) ±rem ±span ±limit)]
  <> {(P): (), rem: →limit, span: 0, (S): (↑S ↑C0), (C): ($tr C0 ())}

;; Self-destruct once exhausted.
C←(wrapper ⍊ nodes: ()) <> {(C): ()}

